-### Edge Cases and Pitfalls
-*   **Joint Color and Opacity Update:** While VDGS can update both color and opacity, ablation studies show that updating only opacity yields more consistently high-quality results on real scenes [S1]. Updating both simultaneously can be less effective due to the dependency of opacity on color [S1].
-*   **Opacity Multiply vs. Color Multiply:** The "Opacity Multiply" variant (VDGS Opacity Multiply) achieves the best scores on real scenes, while "Color Multiply" performs best on the NeRF Synthetic dataset [S1].
-*   **Precision:** The hash grid provides fast queries but requires careful tuning of table size and levels to avoid collisions or insufficient resolution [S1].
+**Parameters:**
+*   **Hash Grid Levels:** 12 [S1].
+*   **Hash Table Size:** $2^{14}$ (16384) [S1].
+*   **Feature Dimension:** 2 [S1].
+*   **MLP Layers:** 2 [S1].
+*   **Activation:** LeakyReLU [S1].
+*   **Training Hardware:** NVIDIA RTX 3090 GPU [S1].
+*   **Implementation Frameworks:** PyTorch and TinyCUDA [S1].
+
+**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
+*   **Joint Color and Opacity Update:** Updating both color and opacity via the neural network simultaneously yields slightly worse results than updating only one. This is attributed to the dependency of opacity on color, making joint training ineffective [S1].
+*   **Opacity vs. Color Update:** Changing only opacity (Opacity Multiply) provides the most consistently high-quality results on real-world scenes. Changing only color (Color Multiply) performs best on the NeRF Synthetic dataset [S1].
+*   **Precision:** The use of hash encoding provides fast queries and superior results compared to frequency encoding for Gaussian means [S1].
